  {
    "episode_id": 96335,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96335.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "on the ballot. So I find inspiration, I find strength in the movement. When it comes to the party, listen, I'm a Democrat. That's all I've ever been. And we are a big tent party. I will say that the struggle for me is to be The weaponized, mischaracterized, boiled down to, in some instances, a shallow digestible soundbite that does not center my humanity, that is not representative of the work that I've put in. I think when you are doing the work of resistance to injustice, when you are actively seeking to dismantle white supremacy, to undo the harm of decades of policy violence. You know, not everyone is going to love that. And so I just accept that this, I'm honoring the tradition of that black women have played on the front lines of every social justice movement. We are truth tellers. You know, we are table shakers. We are also coalition builders. You know, we are conveners and You know, that's not going to be that's not going to be easy. So I don't think I don't always feel understood. And I think to be misunderstood can be a very painful reality.",
