    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "both harm reduction, dismantling, and also seeking to advance things. I say policy is my love language because we've legislated hurt and harm. We can legislate equity. We can legislate healing. We can legislate justice. That's what I seek to do and do that in a way that's intersectional because that's how we live. We don't live in big check boxes. You know, all of these things are connected. We live in intersectionality, complexity, nuance, right? Shout out to Kimberly Crenshaw. So we live in that intersectionality. And so I think there's a defensiveness that many elected officials feel in trying to make the case for like, no, I am trying. I am working. So they just start giving you like a resume. And people are like, OK, I hear that. Some of it I don't. But regardless, I'm still struggling. And we have to center that be real. Don't talk people out of their struggle. You can let them know you're fighting for them. But you need to actively listen. The other thing is we have to have a paradigm shift of politics of transformation, not a transaction. One of the things that we do in our organization is what we call deep canvassing work. It's like really the work of deep democracy, which is we are canvassing and we are knocking doors year round, not just for an election cycle. And we're knocking doors not to say, are you going to vote for me or not? You know, we're engaged in conversation. We're asking, what are your needs? Are your basic needs being met? Oh, do you know about student debt cancelation? That was one of the issues we went on doors with in the middle of the pandemic talking to people about. And we were able to move the dial on that. There was a",
