    "turn_text": "But that's what I'm saying. So there aren't some victories along the way. But my longer point here is that we single issue constituencies, and that does a disservice in every way. So movement building, to telling the story of the impact of the victories that we have had. Because you're like, okay, LGBTQ folks, we used to think they cared about marriage equality. We took care of that, so they're probably all set. Or maybe we could talk a little bit about like public accommodation for trans individuals, but like, we're good. You know, if we speak to Hispanic and Latinos and same for immigration reform, they're good, even though like South Asians experience unjust deportation, separation of family, black immigrants, all of that, and with women. And as long as we say we're pro-choice and we support reproductive justice, they're gonna be all good. And they don't say intersectionality. And they don't say nothing to the AAPI community. I tell my friends in the AAPI community, I'm like, y'all are not even the model minority, you're the invisible one. I mean, no one, but what has happened with the AAPI community, and also this is true for black men, is that in many of these states that are up for grabs that we really need to keep or that we hope that we pick up, they are becoming the margin of victory. The AAPI community in Atlanta and Georgia, we saw that. As Georgia became more purple, the",
